Fey is a specification for a novel markup language, designed to meet the
needs of individual expression. Some of the goals are for it to be easily
extensible via scripting and useable for a wide range of applications from
documentation to configuration files and data serialization.

Fey's core thesis is that markup should look the way we want it to, and so,
where possible, each syntactic feature offers a variety of markup options to
choose from, so far as the grammar permits it.

Some arbitrary choices are made that try to align with expected norms, but
otherwise Fey strives to break away from what one might consider 'standard'.


  I.A. Why scriptable

The experimental idea is that a Fey interpreter can read Fey instructions that
tell it how to interpret new Fey commands (and possibly syntax) that are defined
by the user. This should allow for the language to appear different to different
users, while still adhering to the same specification for a Fey interpreter.

This is where Fey gets its name from. In folklore and fantasy, a fey creature is
one that contains powerful magic, and is often unpredictable.

In the same way, Fey should be able to be reshaped and redefined by its users,
in a way that is unpredictable at first glance, but is well defined by the
instructions which define the additions and/or changes.

We don't know if all of these goals are mutually compatible, but until proven
otherwise we will exhuast all efforts to create a universally applicable markup
langauge.


  I.B. First steps

The current milestone goal is to expressly define the core syntax for fey to be
used in places where Markdown or Org-mode are currently, such as for README
documentation, writing articles for static site generation, or taking notes for
a PKM where the data in the notes is readily indexable for searching and
management.

In conjunction with that goal, a neovim plugin is being developed to make
editing fey files seamless, with features that are comparable to org-mode when
it comes to editing notes, such as moving heading subtrees up/down etc.

[url] https://github.com/Loreite/fey.nvim [Fey Markup Language Neovim Plugin]

Though do no expect for a very long time (if ever) that fey will have feature
parity when it comes to org-mode features like agenda and task tracking, unless
it becomes a clear point of interest for myself or large portion of the
community of users. If those features are currently important and/or
non-negotiable I encourage you to continue to use and support org-mode, and push
for its wider adoption.


  I.C. Why not Markdown or Org-mode

While these markup languages are incredibly valuable for different reasons:

Markdown is a simple specification widely adopted with a large support for
advanced features in different software such as obsidian or the Hugo static site
generator.

Org-mode is a more advanced markup ecosystem with features like an
agenda etc, and while very expressive with property drawers and keywords, they
also have limitations that make them unsuitable for use in Hugo for article
metadata, such as not having proper syntax for nested dictionaries or arrays of
values.

Ultimately, no matter where we turn, we have to give up some ground to use these
markup languages, as they don't cover all the purposes we have without bolting
on YAML frontmatter (a not so terrible solution) for example.

What Fey proposes to do is determine all the ground that has been lost to those
other languages and cover it explicitly while also filling the same roles they
are currently fit for. With everchanging needs in mind this is the reason for
Fey's goal for extensibility, to allow users to define their needs and adapt fey
to meet them where they are, if that goal is as possible as we imagine it to be.


  II. What does Fey look like

You're reading Fey right now. This document is written in standard Fey 0.0.2. As
are all other documents in the Fey repository.

Thus, if you want to get a feel for what kinds of things Fey can do out of the
box, then read on, and pay attention to the syntax and structure of all the
documents.


  III. How to use Fey

For now, this README is the only direct specification of Fey's syntax and features.

  IV. Documentation

Like most markup languages, Fey has a standard syntax for many features that make
writing up a large document easier, with features for Headings, Lists, Tables, etc.

We generally call these features Fey 'objects' or 'Constructs' interchangeably.


  IV.A. Headings

  IV.A.i. Segments, Tokens, Ordered Sets

You've already been reading the syntax for headings, but the formal definition
is that fey uses a '<token><delimiter>' (called a segment) notation for each
level of heading. Where 'token' is a string of characters that match this
pattern /[a-zA-Z0-9_]*/.

Furthermore the token itself is broken up into two parts using this pattern
/([a-zA-Z0-9_]*_)([a-zA-Z0-9]*)/.

If you're unfamiliar with regex, then what this means is that we get two pattern
groups $1 and $2, where $1 looks something like '_' or 'word1_' or 'word1_word2_' etc, and
group $2 simply looks something like '1word'.

Put another way, $1 is called the 'anonymous text', and $2 is called the
'enumeration'. The enumeration is the part of the segment that will be
automatically reindexed. The anonymous text component is a way to add context to
a specific heading level. See some of the examples below for more clarity on
this.

The enumeration component of the token will fall into one of the following
predefined ordered sets (or a set defined by the user):

  -  Capital Roman Numerals
  -  I, II, III, IV, V, VI, VII, VIII, IX, X ...

  -  Lowercase Roman Numerals
  -  i, ii, iii, iv, v, vi, vii, viii, ix, x ...

  -  Capital Letters
  -  A, B, C, ... X, Y, Z, AA, AB, AC, ...

  -  Lowercase Letters
  -  a, b, c, ... x, y, z, aa, ab, ac, ...

  -  Decimal Numbers
  -  1, 2, 3, ... 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, ...

  -  Hexadecimal Numbers
  -  0x1, 0x2, 0x3, ... 0xd, 0xe, 0xf, 0x10, 0x11, 0x12 ...

  -  Binary Numbers
  -  0b1, 0b10, 0b11, ... 0b1010, 0b1011, 0b1100, ...

Each ordered set is defined by its initial value of its ordering. So all
subsequent headings will be ordered at any given depth based on the first token
found at that depth on the page. Plugins should also be configurable to make
them only order at that depth for a given subtree.

This means that the first index value of any ordered set must be unique in order
to be properly distinguised by the fey interpreter.

(It is also possible to undefine an ordered set in order to free up a character)


  IV.A.ii. Delimiters, Anonymity, Prefix

The 'delimiter' is the separator between the tokens, the count of which
determines the depth or level of the heading. The tokens themselves only specify
information about the section ordering.

The 'delimiter' can also be defined by the user to be any character not found
in one of the ordered sets. The current set of delimiters is:

    . , : ; ! ? \ / ' " ` - + * = ~ ^ @ # $ & % [ ] ( ) { } < >

For any given heading segment, the token can be left empty or 'anonymous' (see
below). Altogether the sequence of segments is collectively called the heading
'signature'.


  IV.A.iii. All parts together

Headings are also defined by the specification to begin with two (2) spaces before
the signature followed by a space and then the 'title' which is all the plain-text
until the end of the line.

The leading two spaces help the parser identify headings from standard lines of
text that begin like 'endofasentence. new sentence'. It also creates a clear
visual distinction for a reader of the plain text (when no syntax highlighting
is available) where a heading is relative to its body content.

Other than Lists, no other Fey syntactic objects may be indented two (2) spaces.


***  src Fey Heading Examples
  .... Anonymous heading
  ..i.. This is a partially anonymous heading
  I.1..i. This is also a partially anonymous heading

  I.1.i.ii. "'I.1.i.ii.' is the signature, but everything in quotes is the title"

  ###Example_iii: this heading has anonymous text as part of the level 4 enumeration
  ###Example_iv: the token enumeration updates, but the anonymous text doesn't
                you can se how this would be useful for enumerating headings
                related to a specific topic

  !!!! Note
  !!!! (notice this is a level 4 heading like the rest)
       It is highly recommended that the use of an available Fey plugin or
       extension to your preferred text editor be used to automatically generate
       the correct heading syntax. Such a plugin should increment the ordering
       of the relevant level of heading for new headings, or update the ordering
       of all headings when a heading is moved or deleted.

       See link to fey.nvim above for neovim support.
***


  IV.B. Lists

Fey's ordered and unordered lists are as equally as expressive as headings,
using the same '<token><delimiter>' segment to partially define itself. 
*See #[section IV.A.i.]# for more information on segments.*

The second condition that defines a list is that the segment must be followed by
a mix of two spaces or tabs before the body content/paragraph. This is what
allows lists to be parsed as different from headings, and it also means that
Lists may be indented any number of spaces, unlike other Fey syntactic objects
like Blocks or Tags, etc, which cannot be indented two (2) spaces to be parsed
correctly.

Lists are terminated by either two (2) empty newlines, when content on the
following line that is indented at a lower level than its bullets, or at the
start of a new section because of a heading.

Anonymous lists


  IV.B.i. Unordered Lists

Unordered (or anonymous) Lists are are the most simple; they use an anonymous
segment as their bullet which forgoes having a token:

    :  Each item in an unordered list is preceded by a character from a defined
       set, followed by a space.

       :  The indentation of the bullet determines the level of the list item.


###  src Fey Unordered List Examples
  .  list level 1
     .  level 2
     -  same list new bullet type
  -  [SUBLIST END] new level 1 listitem ends the sublist

  -  same list, just separated by a newline for clarity


  [LIST END] two newlines ends the previous list

  -  new list
  [LIST END] any content not indented to greater than the
             list ends the list as well

  -  new list because of previous paragraph
  * a heading will also immediatly end the list
###


  IV.B.ii. Ordered Lists

Ordered lists are only slightly more complex than unordered lists, since the are
enumerated just like headings. This means they they can use one of the
predefined ordered sets just like heading segments (or one defined by the user).

    1)  Each item in an ordered list is preceded by an enumeration from one of
        the ordered set of characters followed by two (2) spaces.
        (e.g. '1', 'a', 'A', 'i', 'I', '0x1', '0b1', etc.)

        1)  The indentation of the bullet determines the level of the list item

    2)  The indentation of the list item is determined by the number of spaces or tabs before the '.' character.


###  src Fey Ordered List Example
  1.  list level 1
      1.  level 2
      2-  same list new bullet type
  2-  [SUBLIST END] new level 1 listitem ends the sublist

  3-  same list, just separated by a newline for clarity


  [LIST END] two newlines ends the previous list

  1-  new list
  [LIST END] any content not indented to greater than the
             list ends the list as well

  1-  new list because of previous paragraph
  * a heading will also immediatly end the list
###


  IV.B.iii. Mixing List Types and Anonymous Text

Lists actually follow a similar specification as Headings, except that where a
Heading signature can have multiple segments, eg 'II.A.', bullets can only have
one segment, but it can also be anonymous to create an unordered listitem, as we
have seen.

The bullets of lists and their sublists act much like the different segments of
a heading, where each new depth of a sublist is akin to adding a new segment
onto a heading. The only real difference then between a heading and a list is
that lists are hierarchically defined by their indentation.

Unordered listitems can be intermixed with ordered listitems, which has the same
cumulative effect on the following listitems as anonymous segments in a heading
does, ie. it skips it in the ordered count.

For example, the following ordered list is compared to equivalent headings...


###  src Fey Mixed List Example
  I.  same as heading 'I.'
      A.  same as heading 'I.A.'
          i.  same as heading 'I.A.i.'
          ii.  same as heading 'I.A.ii.'

      .  anonymous heading does not affect ordering

      B.  same as heading 'I.B.'
          i.  same as heading 'I.B.i.'

  II.  same as heading 'II.'
      A.  same as heading 'II.A.'
      .  etc...
###


And lastly, just like with heading segments, we can use anonymous text as part
of our bullets (both ordered and unordered) to add some clarity and flexibility
to our lists.


###  src Fey Anonymous Text List Example
  Listitem_:  this is an unordered listitem

  Listitem_:  we can use the same anonymous text for each item

  third_in_list_:  or change it up, depending on our needs

    start_of_sublist_I:  This is an ordered listitem

    second_of_sublist_:  We can still mix unordered items

    third_of_sublist_II:  They keep counting when an enumeration is present
###


  IV.C. Blocks

The standard Block is defined by an opening and closing fence, may have optional
name followed by optional parameters, and possibly contain inner content. The
parameters list must come after a mix of two spaces or tabs. This helps the fey
parser determine what is a fence and what is a heading signature.

If the Block does contain inner content it does NOT respect the indentation of
the text outside of the Block. Indeed the content is indentation agnostic. It
only repsects itself, and what the optional parameters may specify.

Currently, only 'src' named Blocks intentionally supported, and the first
parameter is used to name a filetype which will use an available treesitter
parser to inject syntax highlighting. This will change in the future once the
paramters list specification is more refined to allow the Block to be used for
other more complex features. The lack of a name or filetype just means there is
no syntax highlighting.

Fey Blocks do not contain each other, but, in the case of a Fey src Block, the
parser does correctly recognize Blocks within itself, so long as either the
fences use a different character, a different length, or a deeper indentation
level (min 1 column).


###  src Fey Nested Block Example
  * heading etc...

$$$  src Fey Inner Block: Different Character
  * heading etc...

 $$$  src Fey Inner-Inner Block: Different Indentation
   * heading etc...
 $$$
$$$

####  src Fey Inner Block: Different Length
  * heading etc..
####

###


  IV.C.i. Fences

The opening and closing fences of a Block may appear at any indentation
level, but they must match indentation for the Block to parse correctly.

Fences are nearly as flexible as Heading and List segments, they can be
expressed using a wide list of character options.

Fences must be at least three (3) characters long, but there is no limit to how
long they can be, except normal computing limits in the parser.


| name       | begin | end |
+============+=======+=====+
| underscore | ___   | ___ |
| dash       | ---   | --- |
| tilde      | ~~~   | ~~~ |
| equal      | ===   | === |
| plus       | +++   | +++ |
+============+=======+=====+
| bang       | !!!   | !!! |
| intero     | ???   | ??? |
| dollar     | $$$   | $$$ |
| at         | @@@   | @@@ |
| hash       | ###   | ### |
| percent    | %%%   | %%% |
| ampersand  | &&&   | &&& |
+============+=======+=====+
| asterisk   | ***   | *** |
| caret      | ^^^   | ^^^ |
| grave      | ```   | ``` |
| s-quote    | '''   | ''' |
| d-quote    | """   | """ |
+============+=======+=====+
| b-slash    | \\\   | \\\ |
| f-slash    | ///   | /// |
| dot        | ...   | ... |
| comma      | ,,,   | ,,, |
| colon      | :::   | ::: |
| s-colon    | ;;;   | ;;; |
+============+=======+=====+


  IV.D. Tables

Tables in Fey can be both incredibly simple or complex (in a good way) to best
suit the needs of the user. 

While they can be created manually by typing out each character or doing other
text manipulation, it is recommended that for a Table to contain some of its
more advanced features that you use the built in key mapped operations to
maintain a consistently legible (and parseable) Table.


  IV.D.i. A Simple Table anatomy

All Tables in Fey must start with a plain row called the crown. The cells of the
crown may contain content, but it does not support the more advanced table
features because it serves an essential purpose for parsing Fey Tables: it
determines how many physical columns there are.

Most table operations automatically reformat the Table, but you can use the
neovim convention `gqq` with the cursor inside of a Table to reformat it.


###  src Fey Example Table

This is a 3x3 Table, in order it has...

  1.  Crown Row with content
  2.  Table Section Separator (optional)
  3.  Plain Row with content
  4.  Plain Row without content


  | The   | Crown | Row     |
  +=======+=======+=========+
  | Plain | Row   | Content |
  |       |       |         |
###


  IV.D.ii. Multi-line cells

One thing about ascii-tables is that they can be notoriously difficult to use
and maintain if the content in any cell of the table would be be too long to
stay on the screen, and this compounds for every Table cell this is true for on
the same row.

This is why Fey Tables introduce the concept of the 'logical row' as a separate
construct from the plain row we've already seen. Logical rows begin with a
separating line called the 'cell boundary start'(CBS). This tells both the parser,
and us the user that until we see the line called a 'cell boundary end' (CBE) keep
treating all the plain rows we read as if they belong to the same logical row.

Additionally, if we have multiple consecutive logical rows between the CBS/CBE,
then we delineate them with a 'cell boundary inner' (CBI).

Lets take a look at the anatomy of this now.


###  src Fey Example Table

This is also a 3x3 Table, in order it has...

  1.  Crown Row without content
  2.  Table Section Separator (optional)
  3.  Cell Boundary Start Separator

  4.  Logical Row (all cells in this row are multi-line cells)
      1.  Plain row with content
      2.  Plain row with content

  5.  Cell Boundary End Separator
  6.  Plain row without content


  |              |     |         |
  +==============+=====+=========+
  v--------------v-----v---------v
  | Logical      | row | content |
  | cell content |     |         |
  ^--------------^-----^---------^
  |              |     |         |
###


  IV.D.iii. Horizontally Merged Cells

Another thing about ascii-tables that is a challenge is needing to have data
that spans multiple columns. It can be very limiting to only be able to export
tables with this requirement using embedded HTML. Its not a bad option, but we
end up losing the visual clarity that and ascii-table provides.

Some languages like RST do offer this feature in their tables, but it often
means giving up plain row data, because the parser requires that every row/cell of
data is surrounded by an ascii-box. Thankfully, Fey's existing definition of a
logical row for multi-line cells can be backpacked to support horizontally
merged cells.

To do so, we simply omit the centre beam ('|') between the two cells to be
merged, and replace the 'v'/'+' on either the CBS/CBI repsectively with an
asterisk ('*').

The anatomy of this should look surprisingly simple for an otherwise complicated
task to perform.


###  src Fey Example Table

This is also a 3x3 table, in order it has...

  1.  Crown Row without content
  2.  Cell Boundary Start Separator

  3.  Logical Row (all cells in this row are multi-line cells)
      1.  Plain row with content
      2.  Plain row with content

  4.  Cell Boundary End Separator
  5.  Plain row without content


  |       |       |         |
  v-------*-------v---------v
  | Merge Column  | content |
  | cell content  |         |
  ^-------^-------^---------^
  |       |       |         |
###


  IV.D.iv. Vertically Merged Cells

Just like with horizontally merged cells, we face the same problem with
vertically merged cells. We want it to be both clear to use as the viewer, as
well as precise for the parser/exporter.

Vertically merged cells might be even easier to manage too, since all we must do
is omit the center separator ('~~~~~~~') between two cells in a column. The
space where the separator would have been becomes and active line in the cell
available for containing cell data.

Let's take a look at the anatomy of this now.


###  src Fey Example Table

This is a 3x4 table, in order it has...

  1.  Crown Row without content
  2.  Cell Boundary Start Separator

  3.  Logical Row (all cells in this row are multi-line cells
      1.  Plain row with content

  4.  Cell Boundary Inner Separator

  5.  Logical Row (all cells in this row are multi-line cells
     1.  Plain row without content

  6.  Cell Boundary End Separator
  7.  Plain row without content


  |     |              |         |
  v-----v--------------v---------v
  | row | Merge Row    | content |
  +~~~~~+ cell content +~~~~~~~~~+
  |     |              |         |
  ^-----^--------------^---------^
  |     |              |         |
###


  IV.D.v. Putting it all together

By now I hope you've come to imagine that all these features of a Fey Table
don't have to exist in isolation. In fact they will happily share a table
together like in the example below!

Though I do hope you'll forgive me for not doing another breakdown of this more
complex example


###  src Fey Example Table

| 0 | 1       | 2     | 3   | 4 | 5 | 6      | 7 |
v---v---------*-------v-----v---v---v--------v---v
| 1 | multi-          |     |   |   |        |   |
+~~~+ col             +~~~~~+~~~+~~~+~~~~~~~~+~~~+
| 2 | & row           |     |   |   | multi- |   |
+~~~+~~~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~+~~~~~+~~~+~~~+ row    +~~~+
| 3 |         |       |     |   |   |        |   |
^---^---------^-------^-----^---^---^--------^---^
| 4 | Plain   | row   |     |   |   |        |   |
| 5 | Another | Plain | row |   |   |        |   |
v---v---------*-------*-----*---v---v--------v---v
| 6 | multi-col                 |   |        |   |
^---^---------^-------^-----^---^---^--------^---^
| 7 |         |       |     |   |   |        |   |
###


  V. Features Still In the Desing Process

  V.A. Tags

Tags in Fey are a versatile component of Fey's syntax used for either syntactic
markup, structural markup, or data serialization.

All the pairs of '[], {}, (), <>' are considered the same interchangeable
syntactic unit in accordance with Fey's core thesis about user visual preference.

Line, Block, or paired Open/Close tags can be intermixed. That is, Block tags
can contain Line tags and Open/Close pairs, and Open/Close Pairs can contain
Line and Block tags. They all also contain at a minimum a 'name' and optionally
a list of 'metadata'.

The name can be any single word using these characters [a-zA-Z_-].

The metadata takes the form of a list of 'key: value' or 'key: "value with
spaces"' pairs separated by a ',' (comma).

The symbol (referred to as a tag's 'token') used to denote a Line/Block/Open/Close
tag can be any from the set [# @ & $ % : . / \ ? !]. In the table below we use
'#' (hash) as an example to show how the position of this token defines what
type of tag it is.

| Plain          | Line    | Block   | Open    | Close   |
+================+=========+=========+=========+=========+
| [generic text] | #[name] | [name]# | [name#] | [#name] |
| {generic text} | #{name} | {name}# | {name#} | {#name} |
| (generic text) | #(name) | (name)# | (name#) | (#name) |
| <generic text> | #<name> | <name># | <name#> | <#name> |
+================+=========+=========+=========+=========+


  V.A.i. Plain Tags

The plain tag contains no name or metadata. It is defined as just a pair of
brackets, braces, parentheses, or chevrons with generic inner content that has no
set structure. This makes it available for parentheticals in paragraph text, or
to be used as part of other syntactic structures (see super-/sub-scripts).

Defining it this way allows Fey to refer back to text that is marked up this way
easily as a 'plain tag' elsewhere in this documentation.


  V.A.ii. Line Tags

Line tags are non-nesting inline tags which consumes the text on the same line
after itself as its body up to the next occurence of its token or optionally
until the end of the line.

'''
Non body text #[name key: value, ...] body text# non-body text
Non body text #[name key: value, ...] body text until end of line
'''

The expcted format of the body text is defined by the name, eg. list or map.

Like with plain tags, the tag itself may span multiple lines.
See [ Data Serialization ] below for more information on lists.


  V.A.iii. Block Tags

And then there is the nesting, whitespace indentation respecting tag such as
'#[]' which consumes both the rest of the line after itself as its head, and the
following lines of content which all begin at the next level of indentation as
its body, like so...
'''
[name key: value, ...]# head...
    body...
'''
where the expected head and body format are defined by the initial symbol, ie.
'#' which expects a map.
Like with plain tags, the tag itself may span multiple lines.
See [ Data Serialization ] below for more informiation on maps.


  V.A.iv. Paired Open/Close Tags

Finally, we have nestable paired tags which are like plain tags in that they
only contain a name and metadata. They perform much like structural html or xml
tags, but the symbol used to define them determines what is expected in the body
which is all the content between each tag, and does not respect whitespace.

This adaptation allows fey to be easily translated into html, without needing to
support html embedding, as what would be html can be written directly using
paired tags, like so...

'''
[html/ lang: "en"]
[head/]
    [title/]Home[/title]
    [meta/ charset: "utf-8"]
    [style/]
        body {
            margin: 0%;
            padding: 0%;
            background-color: #221a0f;
        }
        hr {
            color: #ebc295;
        }
        .root {
            display: flex;
            flex-flow: row wrap;
            min-height: 100vh;
        }
    [/style]
[/head]
[body/]
    [header/]
    [/header]
    [div/ class: "root"]
        [div/ class: "menu"]
            [ul/]
                [li/][a/ href: "index.html"]
                        [h3/]Home[/h3]
                    [/a][/li]
                [li/][a/ href: "pages/character.html"]
                        [h3/]Character Sketch[/h3]
                    [/a][/li]
            [/ul]
        [/div]
        [div/ class: "page"]
            [div/ class: "content"]
                [div/ class: "text-content"]
                    [h1/]Home[/h1]
                    [h2/]Welcome[/h2]
                    [p/]
                        Neque, nisi vitae dapibus magna, suscipit eget sed parturient sit. Condimentum
                        nunc non molestie sed aliquam ac sed imperdiet ac. Eget, mi at tellus facilisis
                        tincidunt laoreet nam. Pulvinar risus habitasse varius pulvinar nec vivamus
                        imperdiet nulla bibendum. Eget ornare vivamus, porttitor porta non ante dui
                        lacus porttitor.
                    [/p]
                [/div]
                [div/ class: "footer"]
                    [img| src: "images/some-image.png",
                        height: "740", width: "580",
                        alt: "Nullam aenean, etiam imperdiet imperdiet velit.",
                        style: "display:block; margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto;"]
                    [hr/]
                    [p/]Copyright (c) 2025 Aldo Zeroun[/p]
                [/div]
            [/div]
        [/div]
    [/div]
[/body]
[/html]
'''

  V.B. Superscripts and Subscripts

To indicate superscripts, we can use '^' (caret) between the single 'carrier'
word or number and the superscript text or 'load'. If the carrier or load
contains an '_' (underscore) or the load contains multiple space delimited
words, numbers, or symbols, either side can be surrounded by a Plain Tag (see
Plain Tags) to remove ambiguity.

Similarly, subscripts use a '_' (underscore) between the single carrier and the
load. Plain tags can again be used to remove ambiguity if there are underscores
in the carrier or load, or the load contains multiple space delimited words,
numbers, or symbols.

'''
carrier^load 9.5^4 {10_000_000}^(the moon)
carrier_load 9.5_4 {10_000_000}_(the moon)
'''

Six settings are available to set whether Plain tags are required when using a
live renderer, on export to a staic format, or used by an editor to show pretty
wysiwyg text such as in neovim. When they are true, Plain tags are optional;
when they are false, Plain tags are required. By default they are all true.

    -  fey-render-subscripts
    -  fey-export-subscripts
    -  fey-pretty-subscripts
    -  fey-render-superscripts
    -  fey-export-superscripts
    -  fey-pretty-superscripts

Lastly, the plugin for your editor of choice should provide commands that can
toggle on/off showing wysiwyg super-/sub-scripts individually or together.

In the fey.nvim plugin for example we have:

    -  FeyTogglePrettySuperscript
    -  FeyTogglePrettySubscript
    -  FeyPrettySubSuperscriptOn
    -  FeyPrettySubSuperscriptOff


  V.C. Emphasis

| emphasis      | clr, hl    | style     |
+===============+============+===========+
| _this text_   | fg         | underline |
| !this text!   | fg         | bold      |
| /this text/   | fg         | italic    |
| ~this text~   | fg         | strike    |
+===============+============+===========+
| __this text__ | bg, fg     | underline |
| !!this text!! | bg, fg     | bold      |
| //this text// | bg, fg     | italic    |
| ~~this text~~ | bg, fg     | strike    |
+===============+============+===========+
| $this text$   | red        |           |
| ^this text^   | orange     |           |
| *this text*   | green      |           |
| &this text&   | yellow     |           |
| %this text%   | fuscia     |           |
| \this text\   | blue       |           |
| =this text=   | purple     |           |
| -this text-   | teal       |           |
| +this text+   | bg2        |           |
+===============+============+===========+
| $$this text$$ | bg, red    | highlight |
| ^^this text^^ | bg, orange | highlight |
| **this text** | bg, green  | highlight |
| &&this text&& | bg, yellow | highlight |
| %%this text%% | bg, fuscia | highlight |
| \\this text\\ | bg, blue   | highlight |
| ==this text== | bg, purple | highlight |
| --this text-- | bg, teal   | highlight |
| ++this text++ | bg, fg2    | highlight |
+===============+============+===========+
| #this text#   |            |           |
| ?this text?   |            |           |
| .this text.   |            |           |
| ,this text,   |            |           |
| :this text:   |            |           |
| ;this text;   |            |           |
+===============+============+===========+
| ##this text## |            |           |
| ??this text?? |            |           |
| ..this text.. |            |           |
| ,,this text,, |            |           |
| ::this text:: |            |           |
| ;;this text;; |            |           |
+===============+============+===========+
| `this text`   |            |           |
| 'this text'   |            |           |
| "this text"   |            |           |
+===============+============+===========+
| ``this text`` |            |           |
| ''this text'' |            |           |
| ""this text"" |            |           |
+===============+============+===========+


  V.D. Data Serialization

For data serialization in Fey, we use two specifc data structurs, the list ':'
and the map '#'. These tags can be plain anonymous data, or have their own
name and metadata associated with them. eg. '[name| key value ...]'



  V.E. Actions

|            |   |   |
+============+===+===+
| [[this]]   |   |   |
| {{this}}   |   |   |
| ((this))   |   |   |
| <<this>>   |   |   |
+============+===+===+
| [[[this]]] |   |   |
| {{{this}}} |   |   |
| (((this))) |   |   |
| <<<this>>> |   |   |
+============+===+===+

$$$  misc
the general idea is that a tag can be one of the following things:
- anonymous tag: `[]`
- anonymous tag with subject: `[: subject]` or `[: some subject]`
- named tag: `[tagname]` or `[tag name]`
- named tag with subject: `[tagname: subject]` or `[tag name: some subject]` etc.


additionally, tags can be given properties based on the group of characters which immediate preced or immediately follow the tag
- heading linking
cannot be anonymous (for now at least it is undefined)
the name should be the filename of the document the heading is in
    - '[]#' - for internal links
    - '#[]' - for external links (experimental. will only look in lateral directories or their subdirectories)
- tag linking
cannot be anonymous (for now at least it is undefined)
the name/subject of the link tag must match exactly the name/subject of the tag it is linking to.
- '[]@' - for internal links
- '@[]' - for external links (experimental. will only look in lateral directories or their subdirectories)
- Monospaced text block
the name of the tags should either match, or the closing tag can be anonymous.
the name of the tags inform the interpreter what language syntax highlighting to use, ie. 'zig', 'c', 'python', etc.
    - '[]{' open monospaced text block
    - '}[]' close monospaced text block

- Line-wise tags
- '[]:' - all of the content up to the next newline is considered the body of the tag
- ':[]' - all of the content preceding the tag on the current line is considered the body of the tag
$$$





